wild hearts's Inspirations are obvious  but its unique high-speed crafting  system creates a novel way to ground and  defeat gigantic beasts the easy line to  draw from Wild Hearts the upcoming  hunting game from Publishers EA and koi  Tecmo and developer Omega Force is  capcom's Monster Hunter wild Hearts is a  game about exploring large open areas  alone or with two other players to take  down gigantic monsters that move around  the map as you fight them  and the developers don't entirely shy  away from that comparison but they're  aspiring to do something much more than  just replicate Monster Hunter the games  share many similarities but developer  Omega Force has explored the genre  before with a few games in the toucaden  series in those games like in Monster  Hunter and in Wild Hearts you hunt giant  monsters to harvest them for material  which can be used to upgrade your  equipment despite their lineage in the  genre koi Tecmo is quick to say that  wild Hearts is not an extension of the  toucan World they said quote  unfortunately there is no overlap this  is a completely new IP for us we know  that some of our fans were hoping it  would have some overlap with tukaden but  it doesn't wild hearts and tukuden  unfortunately exist in two separate  universes but wild Hearts has allowed  Omega Force to pick up where it left off  in the genre in a big well-funded way  all while exploring an impressive  crafting system called karakuri that  makes it stand apart wild Hearts has  been in development for four years and  represents an unlikely partnership  between EA and koi Tecmo the former is  an American publisher known  predominantly for its sports games in  the latter is a Japanese publisher that  has had success with Dynasty Warriors  the historical action series wild Hearts  is being published under EA's Originals  label which has birthed successful games  like unravel and 2021's it takes two  despite EA Originals so far doing a good  job of embracing the word original and  Publishing games you wouldn't normally  expect to come from EA it is still  surprising to see the announcement of  wild hearts playing it is also  surprising in some ways and not at all  surprising in other ways we were given  access to an early build that let us  play through the opening of the first  few major combat encounters the  non-surprising part parts of the game  will be familiar to anyone who has  played a hunting game like monster  hunter in recent years movement feels  similar as does the basic combat of the  tutorials the layout of the world is  also familiar with these large and dense  distinct separated areas that can be  accessed from The Hub City and each of  those areas houses a few different  creatures all of that we've seen before  the process of failing one of these  giant creatures referred to as kimono  and wild Hearts has a Cadence I have  also experienced before but there is  plenty that gives the game a unique  identity the main one being the karakuri  system karakuri is the crafting system  and in the fiction of wild Hearts it's a  type of magic that instantly produces  impressive pieces of engineering that  you can use in fascinating ways the  first karakuri you unlock is the ability  to produce a box you can climb on top of  that ejects you into the air you can  quickly generate multiple stacked boxes  scrambled to the top and leap this is  useful for exploring the environment but  it gets really fun when you're fighting  a kimono you can stack the boxes to get  above your opponent and launch a  devastating downward attack use it to  quickly get in the air to dodge an area  of effect attack or even create a  barrier to slow down a rampage in kimono  creating these devices is fast recalling  an expert fortnite Builder and even  during my short time with the game I  unlocked a collection of interesting  ones that were helpful both inside and  outside of combat during my second  kimono encounter I unlocked what I would  best describe as a bounce pad that I  could use to both quickly fire me out of  the way of incoming attacks or shoot me  toward the kimono for added damage after  that fight I unlocked this gigantic rope  arrow that I could Mount into the ground  and shoot off into the distance to  create zip lines to make traveling  around the world fast faster and easier  later game footage also showed walls to  be used in combat devices to let you  Glide from high heights and a lot more  the other nice thing about the karakuri  is they are permanent within your world  as are the karakuri placed by other  players participating in your game which  another added bonus they're usable by  everyone for koe Tecmo crafting was an  early idea the team wanted to integrate  into wild hearts and the first versions  had you preparing the devices before  heading out into the world or finding  them along the way and picking them up  and carrying them with you through  iteration though they found it was much  more fun to make the devices quickly in  the middle of combat optimistically the  karakuri system is the mechanic that I  think will separate wild Hearts from  comparable hunting games and I was  really impressed with how quickly I was  able to integrate the devices into all  the combat scenarios  wild hearts's other major distinguishing  factor is the design of its kimono Omega  forces stated goal for its creatures is  they be a combination of animals and  nature and and by Nature they mean like  the environment the kimono that have  been shared as well as the few that I  fought had clear familiar animal Roots  but they were covered in things like  tree roots and Moss the kimono looked as  though they could curl up in the forest  and be confused for a giant overgrown  Rock which makes them all the more  intimidating when you wake them up and  they start running in your direction  during my combat with the kimono I used  a standard Katana and I also unlocked an  umbrella weapon that when used correctly  allowed me to stay up in the air for  short periods of time to float and Dodge  various attacks there are six other  weapons available in the game that will  each have their own combo systems and  finishing move animations  [Music]  when you defeat a kimono before the  battle truly ends you have this chance  to deliver one final blow with a fancy  animation doing this ends the battle and  also instantly rewards you with all the  spoils of Victory there's no need to  stand over the corpse and manually  collect your winnings koi Tecmo sees  this as a specific design choice and a  distinguishing factor of wild Hearts  they said they wanted the players who  put their lives on the line to be  instantly rewarded you know when they  succeed some other small details we  learned from our time with the game and  speaking with koi Tecmo is wild Hearts  will feature an English dub the footage  of the build you're seeing here featured  Japanese dialogue Cooperative players  will be able to drop into the middle of  fights and the main story campaign will  take about 30 hours to complete but  there will be plenty of side quests to  tackle as well as post-game content that  will extend the game even further koi  Tecmo also is promising full cross Play  support and teased some light farming  mechanics and some fishing mechanics as  well wild Hearts will feel familiar to  fans of hunting games like Monster  Hunter but I walked away impressed with  how big a swing this game is from Koi  Tecmo and EA and Omega Force it doesn't  look or particularly play like any game  the developer or Publishers normally  release outside of tukaden and I am  excited to experiment more with the  karakuri system it's the mechanic that  will help wild Hearts stand out from the  crowd and I enjoyed the process of  scrambling up a stack of boxes I built  and leaping off the top to bring down a  powerful blow on a rampage in kimono I  imagine I will be doing it a lot more  with different karakori devices on  February 17th of next year when it  releases for PlayStation 5 Xbox series X  and PC
 

